"""Plain technical introductions for observed subjects; evidence stays separate."""
import re

# These introductions describe the shared technical issue. They are not inferred
# paper results. Match the specific issue before a broader application family.
TOPICS = [
 (r'graph-learning-for-eda', 'Learning from circuit components and their connections',
  'A circuit can be represented as a graph: components or signals are objects, and connections describe how they interact. A graph-learning method uses that connected structure when predicting circuit properties or proposing design choices. The intended benefit is to retain relationships that an unordered list of component features would omit.',
  'Check which circuit properties are supplied to the model and what it predicts or changes. Evaluate on circuits outside the training set and verify selected designs using the relevant design tools. Prediction accuracy and improvement in a completed design are separate measurements; both need their own comparison.'),
 (r'precision|approximate|quantiz', 'Representing numbers with limited precision',
  'Hardware stores numbers using a finite number of bits. Fewer bits can reduce memory transfers and the size of arithmetic circuits. The resulting values can differ from a higher-precision calculation, and those differences can accumulate through a program.',
  'The design choice is which values and operations can use less precision while meeting the required answer quality. Some values may need extra precision or a separate correction. Compare speed and energy at the same application-quality target and include conversion and correction costs.'),
 (r'sparse|irregular|graph-learning', 'Using structure that is uneven or incomplete',
  'A sparse calculation contains values or operations that need not be processed. A graph represents objects and their connections; different objects can have very different numbers of connections. Avoiding unnecessary work can save computation, but locating useful work and distributing it across hardware also takes time.',
  'The choice is how to represent the useful entries or connections and when to process them. Check the cost of indexing, scheduling, and gathering data as well as arithmetic. An evaluation should vary the amount and arrangement of useful work; a favorable average can hide workers waiting for the busiest worker.'),
 (r'quantum', 'Controlling and evaluating quantum operations',
  'A quantum program requires physical operations on quantum states and a procedure for measuring an output. The permitted operations and connections depend on the hardware. Imperfect operations and measurement errors can change the answer, so the mapping from program to device must account for those limits.',
  'Separate the cost of translating the program, executing the quantum operations, and performing classical control or correction. A reduction in a circuit-resource count is evidence about that resource. Establishing application benefit also requires the stated error assumptions, repetition count, and comparison computation.'),
 (r'neuromorphic|non-von|nonvon|emerging-and', 'Changing how a physical system represents computation',
  'Conventional processors repeatedly fetch instructions and operate on stored values. Alternative computing organizations can encode state and updates in different physical structures or event-driven operations. The first question is how the application input is represented and how a usable answer is recovered.',
  'A method can reduce one repeated operation while adding input encoding, control, readout, or error correction. Evaluate that entire sequence at the required output quality. Different alternative devices need different accuracy and resource measures; their shared category does not make their results interchangeable.'),
 (r'photonic|optical', 'Computing or communicating with light',
  'Optical components encode and transform information using light. A system must create the optical signal, control how it passes through components, and detect the result. The digital program around those components can still require data conversion, storage, and control.',
  'Compare the complete input-to-output operation, including conversion, calibration, and control energy. State the required precision and the effect of component variation or temperature. An optical component measurement establishes its behavior under the measured conditions; it does not alone establish a faster application.'),
 (r'analog|rf-and', 'Operating on continuously varying signals',
  'Analog circuits process signals whose values vary continuously over a range. Their behavior depends on component values, noise, signal frequency, and manufacturing variation. A circuit must satisfy its requirements over the intended operating range, rather than at only one nominal input.',
  'Design choices include circuit structure, component sizes, calibration, and physical layout. Evaluate error, usable frequency range, energy, area, and variation as appropriate to the circuit. Automation should report the cost of finding a valid circuit and verify the final result with a sufficiently accurate model or measurement.'),
 (r'3d|thermal|power-integrity', 'Keeping electrical and thermal conditions within limits',
  'Active circuits consume current and generate heat. Supply connections have finite resistance, and heat must pass through physical material before reaching the cooling system. Concentrating activity in one location can therefore change both supply voltage and temperature.',
  'The design can change placement, connections, cooling assumptions, or the timing of activity. Compare designs under the same external supply and cooling conditions. State whether temperatures and voltage changes are measured or modeled, and whether the model captures interaction between components.'),
 (r'chiplet|packag|wafer-scale|heterogeneous-integration', 'Dividing a system across physical components',
  'Splitting computation among components changes where values are produced and consumed. Data crossing a component boundary needs a physical connection and communication control. These use space and energy and may limit the rate at which the components can cooperate.',
  'Partitioning chooses what stays together and what communicates across a boundary. Test the combined system with link capacity, delay, power delivery, temperature, and workload placement stated. Savings in the cost or size of one component must be assessed alongside communication and assembly costs.'),
 (r'placement|routing|floorplan|physical-design', 'Choosing locations and wire paths',
  'A physical circuit needs legal locations for its components and paths for its connections. Components and wires compete for limited space. A shorter path can help timing, but placing more components nearby can make routing difficult or increase heat and current demand.',
  'The method changes the search, representation, or construction of this geometry. Compare completed layouts under the same design rules and timing targets. Report unresolved connections and rule violations as well as runtime and quality; a fast partial layout is a different result from a completed valid layout.'),
 (r'timing|parasitic|characterization', 'Estimating when signals arrive',
  'Circuit operation depends on signals arriving within specified time windows. Devices and wires introduce delay; neighboring structures and operating conditions can change it. A timing or electrical model estimates that behavior so a designer can determine whether the circuit meets its requirements.',
  'The method may make the estimate faster or more accurate. Measure both properties against a stated reference. Check whether estimation errors change a design decision near its limit. Average prediction accuracy alone can conceal the few errors that cause an invalid design to pass.'),
 (r'manufactur|process|device-model|emerging-memory|emerging-device', 'Accounting for how a device is fabricated',
  'Manufacturing creates physical devices with variation in dimensions and material properties. Those differences can change behavior, and defects can prevent correct operation. A device model describes selected effects so that design choices can be evaluated before or alongside fabrication.',
  'Identify which properties are measured, which are estimated, and the conditions used to calibrate the model. For yield claims, specify the population and acceptance criteria. A good match on calibration examples does not establish accuracy for a different device geometry or manufacturing condition.'),
 (r'formal|equivalence', 'Checking behavior against a stated property',
  'Formal checking expresses the design and its requirement in a mathematical model. A checker seeks a behavior that violates the requirement or establishes that no such behavior exists within its assumptions. Equivalence checking compares the behavior of two representations of a design.',
  'The result depends on the modeled states, inputs, and environment. Record the property, assumptions, proof outcome, counterexamples, and unresolved runs. A timeout is not evidence of correctness. Compare methods on the same properties and resource budget, including whether their answers can be independently checked.'),
 (r'verification|validat|debug|simulation|emulation|prototyp', 'Executing and checking design behavior',
  'A test applies inputs to a design model and compares the resulting behavior with an expected result. Simulation executes the model in software; emulation uses another hardware system to execute a representation. Debugging traces an observed failure to a cause that can be changed.',
  'Faster execution allows more tests, but the tests still need to reach meaningful behavior and detect wrong results. State how expected outputs are obtained, which faults are known, which behaviors are covered, and what remains unresolved. Evaluate useful errors found as well as total tests or execution speed.'),
 (r'side-channel|security|secure|trusted|confidential', 'Specifying what an attacker can observe or change',
  'A security claim starts with protected information or behavior and an attacker with stated capabilities. Even when direct access is blocked, shared hardware can expose timing or other signals. A protection changes the available observations, access, or sharing, often with a performance cost.',
  'Evaluate the actual attacker model and record remaining information leakage, attack success, false alarms, and overhead where relevant. A detection method needs both malicious and ordinary cases. A protection against one observation or access path cannot establish security against all others.'),
 (r'privacy', 'Computing while restricting access to protected data',
  'A computation may need to use data that should remain hidden from some participants. The design must specify who can see inputs, intermediate values, and outputs. Protecting that information can require extra computation, communication, or restrictions on the operations that can be performed.',
  'State the privacy requirement and what information the protocol intentionally reveals. Compare full execution at the same protection level, including preparation, communication, and result recovery. A faster arithmetic component establishes only one part of the total protected computation.'),
 (r'reliab|fault', 'Detecting and recovering from incorrect state',
  'Stored state or a computation can become incorrect when a fault occurs. Detection requires enough additional information to distinguish the incorrect state from valid behavior. Correction or recovery requires further information, repeated work, or a preserved earlier state.',
  'Specify the kinds of faults, their locations, and the assumptions about how often they occur together. Measure undetected failures, recovery time, and useful work after protection is enabled. Include the cost of checking in normal operation. Fault-injection results apply first to the injected conditions.'),
 (r'llm-assisted|generative-design|learned|optimization-and-search|graph-and-hypergraph', 'Choosing among many possible designs',
  'A design problem usually admits many candidates, and evaluating every candidate can be too expensive. A search method decides which candidates to try. A learned or generative method uses previous examples or evaluation feedback to propose candidates or predict their quality.',
  'Distinguish proposing a candidate from checking that it works. Include training, prediction, failed attempts, and final verification in the total budget. Compare valid final outputs on unfamiliar tasks. A faster search is useful when its saved evaluations exceed the cost of its own decisions and errors.'),
 (r'synthesis|compil|hardware-generation|cross-layer|architecture-model', 'Turning a program or design into an executable implementation',
  'A design description can be transformed into lower-level operations, a schedule, or a circuit. Each transformation must preserve the required behavior while choosing where and when work happens. The representation determines which dependencies and resource constraints the tool can inspect.',
  'State what the input language permits, which transformations are supported, and how correctness is checked. Measure tool runtime separately from the performance of its output. Compare under the same target hardware and include cases where the tool cannot generate a valid implementation.'),
 (r'reproducib|benchmark', 'Making an experiment repeatable',
  'An experiment needs inputs, a method, a rule for measuring the outcome, and a comparison. Another researcher needs enough information to run those steps and determine whether the same claim follows. A benchmark collects tasks and defines how their outcomes are scored.',
  'Document versions, settings, resource limits, expected outputs, and treatment of failures. Check whether task selection favors a particular method and whether development examples appear in evaluation. Running provided software is an initial check; reproducing a paper result requires the corresponding experiment.'),
 (r'energy|power-and|efficient-compute', 'Reducing energy for completed useful work',
  'A system consumes energy while switching circuits, storing state, transferring values, and waiting with resources active. Reducing instantaneous power can increase completion time. Energy per completed job therefore depends on both operating power and how long the job takes.',
  'The method can change precision, scheduling, activity, or hardware operation. Compare at a fixed workload and required output quality or deadline. Measure the full execution interval and state which components are included. A power rating or isolated operation cost is insufficient to determine application energy.'),
 (r'memory|storage|movement|locality|interconnect|network', 'Controlling where data resides and how it moves',
  'An operation must receive its inputs before it can execute. Memory has limited capacity, and a communication path has limited transfer rate. Requests from different operations can compete for the same path. Keeping a reusable value close to its next user can avoid transfers, but consumes scarce nearby storage.',
  'The method changes placement, reuse, routing, scheduling, or computation location. Measure all transfers and coordination, including initial loading and later migration. Vary input size and competing work. Compare at equal output quality and distinguish transfer savings from complete application speed or energy.'),
 (r'llm-systems|distributed|datacenter|cloud', 'Serving work on shared or distributed resources',
  'A service receives jobs whose arrival times and sizes vary. Multiple jobs share processors, memory, and communication paths; distributing a job adds coordination between machines. The time a user waits includes queueing and communication as well as computation.',
  'The method changes scheduling, resource assignment, or the organization of shared state. Compare throughput and slow-request latency under the same request distribution and quality target. Include resource startup, data loading, and failures when relevant. A gain at one load level may disappear when a different resource becomes limiting.'),
 (r'edge|embedded|mobile|robotic|real-time|multimedia|sensing|signal|bio|scientific', 'Meeting an application requirement under deployment limits',
  'An application defines useful output and the conditions under which it is needed. Input data can vary in size, timing, or quality. Deployment can impose response deadlines, energy limits, limited memory, or restricted communication. These conditions determine which system improvement is useful.',
  'Describe the actual inputs, output-quality rule, timing requirement, and hardware. Include preprocessing, communication, and decision or output generation. Evaluate representative difficult inputs and changes in demand. A faster internal operation supports an application claim only when the complete result still meets its requirements.'),
 (r'accelerator|architecture|dataflow|reconfigurable|cell-and', 'Arranging hardware around the operations it must perform',
  'Computation consists of operations with data dependencies. Hardware assigns resources to those operations and provides paths for their inputs and outputs. A specialized organization can perform useful work concurrently or retain intermediate values, but only if the input structure supports those choices.',
  'Specify which operations are accelerated and which still run elsewhere. Include data supply, control, unsupported operations, and the cost of moving between execution units. Compare complete workloads at the same output quality and account for memory capacity, hardware area, and energy.'),
]

def explain(subtheme):
    for pattern, title, cause, comparison in TOPICS:
        if re.search(pattern, subtheme):
            return ['## ' + title, '', cause, '', comparison, '']
    return []
